Project: ReframeX – M2O Intelligence, for the Wells Fargo M2O Hackathon ('A Utility for Objective Refactoring').
Goal: a reusable, evidence-driven assessment accelerator that analyzes mainframe application artifacts and reports Migration Complexity, Assessment Confidence and Migration Readiness separately, with traceable evidence, dependency graph, missing-information reporting, recommendations and human review. It does NOT convert, containerize or migrate anything.

Non-negotiable rules:
1. Never send source code or artifacts to any public or unapproved external service. The tool makes no outbound network calls by default.
2. Every material conclusion is classified Evidence-based, Inferred, Assumed or Unknown and cites its artifact, line/record, and rule.
3. Missing evidence is never evidence of simplicity. Unsupported artifacts are recorded and reduce confidence; never silently ignored.
4. Complexity, Confidence and Readiness are computed independently. No random, hardcoded or AI-generated numeric scores.
5. Do not assume OpenShift is the only target. Never present a migration pattern as final.
6. Never fabricate components, dependencies, source locations or results. Do not claim full COBOL semantic analysis; this is static extraction.
7. Preserve original artifacts, rule/model/parser versions and reviewer changes (stored separately from machine findings).
8. Stack: React + TypeScript + Vite, Tailwind, lucide-react, React Router, Recharts, React Flow (@xyflow/react); Python FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, SQLite, Pydantic. No Docker, no paid services, no Supabase/Firebase, no unnecessary dependencies.
9. Continue the existing project. Preserve working functionality. Stop at the end of the current phase.